sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Trabecular alignment arises because a trabecula laid down along a pressure or tension line is undisturbed, since there is no shear along those lines, whereas one lying obliquely is acted on by shearing force and displaced until it comes into line with the stress.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Shear removes obliquely laid trabeculae, leaving those aligned with stress lines";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XVI, “On Form and Mechanical Efficiency”; topic “Trabecular architecture and stress lines”. Scope: formation of trabecular bone (and by extension fibre alignment in plants)"
}
